兵，可以去拦截它。 } 40. Rf8 f6 { [%emt 0:00:25] [%think 1786] [%out 1947]
[%in 2] [%cacheread 0] [%cachewrite 2031] Black is almost certainly lost here.
I only have a king and three pawns against a rook and pawns, so the aim is to
lose as little material as possible. The rook on f8 attacks f7. If I play f6,
the pawn on g7 protects it. White's likely reply is Rf7+ followed by Rxg7,
which costs me one pawn. After that, f6 and h6 remain, and f6 still covers g5.
Any other move loses f7 immediately, and usually with check. } 41. Rf7+ Kb6 {
